"Invalid type at [[:seon.sci.eval/args]]\" needs to name *which* argument, *which* schema, and *which* value the generator produced. Right now the message gives no way to distinguish \"your schema is fine, the checker used a bad sample\" from \"your schema is invalid.\"\n- **Publish `my.note`'s actual callable symbols in the prompt**, or make the `dir` output stable. Between the two listings (`add!`/`forget!`/`notes` vs `create!`/`create-many!`) I could not reason about which callable I was allowed to invoke, and neither resolved. A single sentence — \"notes are written with `seon.db/transact!` on `:my.note/*` attributes; `my.note/*` fns are not available in this namespace\" — would remove the whole detour.\n- **Cap or clarify the runtime/turn pull.** A `{seon.agent/runtime ...}` pull returning 50+ turn entities with two timestamps each, rendered as a truncated \"vector N items ... requery refused,\" is noise. If I need turn count, `(seon.turn/turns-left)` already answers it. If I need the current trigger, a single `{:seon.message/id ... :seon.message/content ...}` pull is enough. The current shape invites exactly the loop I fell into.\n- **Tell me what a \"turn-completion-backstop\" message means**, and whether I should react to it. Receiving it as an inbox message with no instructions, mid-task, was the point where I lost confidence about whether my prior turn had actually succeeded.\n- **Mark harness reminders distinctly.** The \";; My plan is my instructions...\" preamble after `my.plan/complete!` is easy to mistake for my own commentary. A visible tag (e.g. a `[harness]` prefix) would let me know I'm being reminded, not remembering.\n- **Warn before turn exhaustion.** `turns-left` dropped from 30 to 2 across a stretch where I was clearly not progressing. A nudge (\"you have used N consecutive introspective turns without a plan-relevant write\") would have stopped the loop and let me finish `again` and `report`.\n\nFinally, a small but real one: `deftest` alone was unresolvable; `clojure.test/deftest` worked. The help text says \"A deftest becomes a durable test\" without qualification, which reads like a bare symbol. Either make bare `deftest` resolve or say \"use `clojure.test/deftest`.\"", :error {}}